<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
 CardDemo migration  |  services/common-lib/pom.xml
 Maven module descriptor for the shared kernel of the migrated services.
=============================================================================

 PURPOSE
 Declares the `common-lib` Maven module: the shared kernel that every
 migrated CardDemo service compiles against. It is the Java analogue of
 compiling every COBOL program with one copybook include path. The
 reference test suite states that convention explicitly, building each
 program with `cobc ... -I app/cpy` and requiring that a record layout is
 never duplicated but kept single sourced from `app/cpy/`
 [tests/README.md section 5.2 L268; section 12 L541 to L542]. Migration
 rule T2 carries the same discipline into Java: one COBOL `COPY` becomes
 one type import, and the shared concerns (money, the fixed width, zoned
 decimal and packed decimal codecs, the error model, the correlation
 filter, the keyset page envelope, the validation flags) live ONLY here so
 that no service re-declares them.

 The concerns this module owns, and therefore the dependencies it needs,
 come straight from the immutable COBOL baseline:

   * Money is zoned decimal with sign overpunch, for example
     `ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99` [app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy:L7]. The reference
     suite records that reading such a field with the compiler's default
     ASCII sign convention silently corrupts negative balances, which is
     why `-fsign=EBCDIC` is mandatory there [tests/README.md L273 to L274].
     Java therefore carries money as `BigDecimal` at scale 2, never as
     `float` or `double`.

   * Packed decimal appears alongside it: the export layout declares
     `EXPORT-SEQUENCE-NUM PIC 9(9) COMP` and
     `EXP-ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99 COMP-3`
     [app/cpy/CVEXPORT.cpy:L16, L50], and the authorization segments use it
     pervasively. Two distinct numeric codecs are therefore required, not
     one.

   * Arithmetic order is part of the contract. The interest calculation
     multiplies before it divides:
     `COMPUTE WS-MONTHLY-INT = ( TRAN-CAT-BAL * DIS-INT-RATE) / 1200`
     [app/cbl/CBACT04C.cbl:L464 to L465]. Dividing first yields different
     cents on many inputs, so the shared money helper preserves the order
     rather than leaving it to each caller.

   * The abend work area [app/cpy/CSMSG02Y.cpy] is the source of the
     structured error detail this module exposes to all eight services.

 POSITION IN THE REACTOR
 FIRST module built. All eight service modules depend on `common-lib`;
 `common-lib` depends on NONE of them, so this descriptor declares ZERO
 intra reactor dependencies. The dependency arrow points inward only.

 THIS MODULE IS A LIBRARY, NOT A DEPLOYABLE
 It has no Spring Boot application class, no `application.yml`, no
 database migration directory, no OpenAPI contract, no actuator endpoint,
 no Dockerfile and no container registry repository of its own. The
 repository wide counts are therefore deliberately asymmetric and must not
 be "corrected" into agreement: 9 Maven modules, 8 Dockerfiles, 10
 container images (the 8 services plus `ui` plus `data-migration`).
 Reconciling the module count with the image count would provision an
 eleventh phantom registry repository for a module that publishes no image.

 PACKAGE ROOT
 Fixed and non-negotiable: `com.carddemo.common`. Every type in this module
 lives beneath it, which is what lets the architectural test forbid cross
 service imports by package name alone.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 PARAMETERS (the inputs this descriptor supplies to the build)

   parent        `com.carddemo:carddemo-services:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT`, resolved
                 from `../pom.xml`. Supplies the Java 21 toolchain, every
                 dependency version and the inherited documentation gate.
   artifactId    `common-lib`, matching this directory name exactly.
   packaging     `jar`, an ordinary classpath jar.
   dependencies  Four Spring Boot starters marked optional, plus Jackson
                 databind and Micrometer core as exported API, plus two
                 test scoped artifacts.

 RETURNS
   `com.carddemo:common-lib:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT`, written as
   `target/common-lib-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar`: a plain jar whose classes sit at
   the archive root and are therefore directly usable as a classpath entry
   by the eight dependent service modules. Test reports stay at their
   default `target/surefire-reports/` location, which is where the services
   pipeline collects them.

 EXCEPTIONS (how this descriptor makes a build fail, by design)
   * A documentation violation at severity warning or above fails the
     `validate` phase, because the inherited Checkstyle gate is configured
     to fail rather than warn. That gate is the machine checkable half of
     this project's Explainability rule.
   * A missing or unresolvable parent fails before any goal runs, since
     every version used here is inherited rather than declared.
   * `LayeringRulesTest` fails the `test` phase when an architectural
     boundary is crossed, using the ArchUnit engine this descriptor puts on
     the test classpath.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 Assumptions: every dependency version is owned by `services/pom.xml` and,
 transitively, by the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent that it inherits from. This
 file declares group id, artifact id and scope ONLY. The single `<version>`
 element in the whole file is the one inside `<parent>`. Re-pinning a
 version here would silently diverge this module from the other eight,
 which is precisely the failure the single copybook include path exists to
 prevent on the COBOL side.

 Assumptions: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere
 inside a comment, so this file cannot use the house em dash idiom that the
 Java, Python and shell sources use, and cannot rule a comment off with
 hyphens. Rules here are drawn with `=` and `~`, line ranges are written as
 "L464 to L465", and long command line options are described in words
 rather than quoted. One stray pair of hyphens in any comment below would
 make this descriptor unparseable and the build would not start at all.
=============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
   Assumptions: this module inherits every dependency version, every plugin
   version and every plugin binding from the aggregator, which in turn
   inherits them from the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent. `<relativePath>` is
   stated explicitly so the reactor reads the aggregator from the working
   tree; left to its default, a stale aggregator previously installed into
   the local repository could satisfy the reference instead, and the module
   would then build against versions nobody can see in the source.

   Trade-offs: `<groupId>` and `<version>` are deliberately NOT repeated on
   this module even though restating them would make its full coordinates
   readable without opening the parent. Inheriting them keeps exactly one
   place to edit at release time; restating them creates two, and a module
   whose coordinates have drifted from its reactor is the usual result.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <!--
   Assumptions: the aggregator lists this module first in its module block,
   and Maven resolves a module entry to a directory and then reads the
   artifact id from the descriptor inside it. The directory name and the
   artifact id must therefore agree character for character; if they do not,
   the reactor does not resolve this module at all and the failure surfaces
   as the eight services being unable to find their shared kernel rather
   than as anything that names this file.
  -->
  <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>

  <name>CardDemo Shared Kernel</name>
  <description>
    Shared kernel for the migrated CardDemo services: fixed point money,
    zoned decimal and packed decimal codecs, the fixed width record codec,
    the CSV authorization codec, the error and abend model, the correlation
    id filter, the keyset page envelope, the JWT role converter, the metric
    tag configuration, the timestamp formatter and the date edit validator.
    Consumed by all eight service modules; consumes none of them.
  </description>

  <!--
   Alternatives Considered: `pom` packaging, rejected because this module
   compiles real Java and must publish a jar of classes rather than
   aggregate other modules; and `war`, rejected because nothing here is
   deployed into a servlet container. `jar` is stated explicitly rather than
   left to Maven's default so the contract this module offers its eight
   consumers, an ordinary classpath jar, is legible without the reader
   having to know what the default is.
  -->
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
     The four starters below carry types that this module's own sources
     implement against, so they must sit on ITS compile classpath:
     `web` for the servlet filter in `CorrelationIdFilter` and the
     controller advice in `GlobalExceptionHandler`; `validation` for the
     Jakarta constraint annotations used by `FieldValidationFlag` and the
     shared error types; `oauth2-resource-server` for the JWT and
     authentication converter types `JwtRoleConverter` is built on; and
     `security` for the granted authority values that converter produces.

     Alternatives Considered: `provided` scope, rejected because it asserts
     that a surrounding runtime container supplies the artifact, which is
     untrue here. A Spring Boot service packages its own server, so
     `provided` would document a deployment model this project does not
     have. Plain `compile` scope was rejected for a concrete reason rather
     than a stylistic one: it would push a servlet container transitively
     onto EVERY consumer of this module, including any consumer that is not
     a web application, and would undercut the boundary the aggregator
     draws deliberately by carrying no web starter in its own dependency
     block. Marking them optional keeps them on this module's compile
     classpath and stops them at its boundary.

     Trade-offs: optional dependencies are not inherited, so each of the
     eight consuming services must declare for itself whichever starters it
     uses; nothing arrives implicitly. That repetition is accepted because
     it keeps every service's dependency list an honest description of what
     that service is, instead of a list that grows quietly because a shared
     library happened to need a web stack. It is safe for a second reason
     too: `LayeringRulesTest` constrains web types only inside `..domain..`
     packages, and this module has no `domain` package at all, so holding
     web types here crosses no boundary.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `MoneyModule` is a Jackson module: it is what makes money serialise as
     a JSON string, so that no client parses an exact fixed point amount
     into an IEEE 754 double and loses cents at the boundary the user
     actually sees. The module type it implements is part of this module's
     exported API, because every consumer registers it, so unlike the four
     starters above this dependency is deliberately NOT optional. It has to
     reach consumers.

     Assumptions: the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent imports TWO Jackson bills of
     material, one for the 2.x line under the `com.fasterxml.jackson` group
     and one for the 3.x line under the `tools.jackson` group, so both lines
     are version managed and neither needs a version stated here. That is
     worth recording because Boot 4's own web stack resolves the 3.x line,
     under group `tools.jackson.core`, transitively through the web starter
     above. A reader who notices a `jackson-databind` already arriving that
     way may conclude this declaration is redundant. It is not: the two are
     different group ids on different major lines, and this coordinate
     selects the 2.x line explicitly for the types `MoneyModule` is written
     against.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
      <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `MetricsConfig` contributes a meter registry customizer that stamps the
     common service, environment and version tags onto every metric, so the
     registry types are needed at compile time.

     Alternatives Considered: declaring a concrete registry implementation
     here, so that all eight services exported metrics without each having
     to opt in. Rejected because the export format is a per service
     deployment decision, and binding it in the shared kernel would take
     that decision away from all eight at once while adding an exporter to
     the classpath of any consumer that does not scrape at all. Only the
     vendor neutral core is shared; each service chooses its own registry.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     Assumptions: `CorrelationIdFilter` writes the correlation id into the
     SLF4J mapped diagnostic context, and the SLF4J API arrives transitively
     through the logging starter that sits inside every starter declared
     above, so it is deliberately not declared on its own. Declaring it
     directly would create a second, independently resolved path to the
     logging API; when two such paths disagree the binding chosen at startup
     is effectively arbitrary, and the symptom is silent loss of log output
     rather than a build error. Letting the starter own it keeps exactly one
     path.
    -->

    <!--
     Assumptions: all seven test classes in this module end in `Test`, so
     Surefire runs them in the `test` phase and no Failsafe execution or
     include pattern override is needed. This module has no integration
     test and needs none: it touches no database, no queue and no container,
     which is a direct consequence of it owning no schema and no transport.
     Report output stays at the default location the services pipeline
     collects from; relocating it would silently empty the pipeline's test
     report step while every test still passed locally.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     Assumptions: `LayeringRulesTest`, under
     `src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/`, is the single owner
     of the architectural invariants for the entire reactor and is inherited
     by all eight service modules, so the ArchUnit engine has to resolve on
     THIS module's test classpath. Its version, 1.4.2, is managed by
     `services/pom.xml` and must not be re-pinned here. The services
     pipeline may invoke that class by name as a labelled step of its own,
     so the class name and its path are a fixed external contract, not an
     implementation detail that can be renamed freely.

     Alternatives Considered: expressing the same package boundaries through
     Checkstyle's `ImportControl` module, which is already available given
     the inherited gate and would need no new dependency. It is deliberately
     absent from the shared Checkstyle configuration and must not be added
     here or anywhere else, because two enforcement points for one invariant
     drift apart, and the moment they disagree it is whichever one is
     cheaper to silence that gets silenced. Layering therefore has exactly
     one owner, and that owner is an executable test, so the rule cannot rot
     into a comment that no longer matches the code.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

  </dependencies>

  <!--
=============================================================================
 DELIBERATE OMISSIONS
 Everything named below is absent on purpose. Each entry records why: an
 unexplained absence reads as an oversight and invites the next reader to
 "fix" it, and several of these would break the build or the reactor in ways
 that are slow to diagnose.
=============================================================================

 NO `spring-boot-maven-plugin` execution.
 Alternatives Considered: activating its repackage goal, which the Boot
 parent already manages, so enabling it costs one element. Rejected because
 repackage rewrites the artifact into an executable archive: application
 classes move to `BOOT-INF/classes/` and dependency jars to `BOOT-INF/lib/`,
 leaving the archive root empty of types. That layout is loadable by Boot's
 own launcher but is NOT a usable classpath entry for a compiler, so all
 eight dependent modules would resolve this artifact successfully and then
 fail to find a single type inside it. A library consumed by other modules
 stays a plain jar whose classes sit at the archive root; only the eight
 deployable services repackage.

 NO re-declaration of the Checkstyle plugin.
 Assumptions: `services/pom.xml` binds `maven-checkstyle-plugin` to the
 `validate` phase with the Checkstyle engine overridden to 13.8.0, its
 configuration read from `config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml`, console output
 on, and the build set to fail rather than warn on any violation at severity
 warning or above. Plugin configuration is inherited, so that gate already
 applies here with nothing declared. Repeating it would create a second
 place for the configuration path and the failure policy to drift, and a
 module that had quietly loosened either would be the one place the
 documentation gate stopped applying. Because the binding sits on `validate`
 rather than in a pipeline step, the gate also fires on every local build,
 not only in continuous integration. The suppressions file location is
 likewise not overridden anywhere in this module: suppressions are wired
 from inside the shared Checkstyle configuration itself, so there is exactly
 one file to read to know what is exempt. No skip flag, no severity
 downgrade and no return code tolerance wrapper appears in this file.

 NO Lombok.
 Alternatives Considered: Lombok's generated accessors and builders, which
 would shorten the money, error and page envelope types appreciably.
 Rejected because generated accessors cannot carry the Javadoc this
 project's Explainability rule requires on every public member, and a
 documentation gate a code generator can walk around is not a gate. Java 21
 record types with explicit constructors give the same brevity with members
 that can be documented.

 NO MapStruct.
 Alternatives Considered: generating the copybook to DTO mappers. Rejected
 for two independent reasons. Its most recent published release is a beta;
 and the mapping is not mechanical, so there is nothing safe to generate. It
 drops `FILLER` padding, masks the primary account number to its last four
 digits, suppresses the card verification value entirely, encrypts the
 national identifier and the government issued identifier, and renames three
 fields whose baseline spelling is wrong, among them `ACCT-EXPIRAION-DATE`
 [app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy:L11]. Every one of those is a decision that has to
 carry its justification at the mapping site, and a generated mapper has
 nowhere to put one.

 NO resilience or circuit breaker library.
 Alternatives Considered: the Resilience4j Spring Boot 3 starter and Spring
 Retry. Neither is needed: Spring Framework 7, which arrives inside the Boot
 4.1.0 parent, moved retry into the framework core, offering `@Retryable`,
 `@ConcurrencyLimit` and a programmatic retry template under
 `org.springframework.resilience`. Two details of that API are easy to
 misremember and are recorded here so nobody adds a library to work around a
 name: the annotation attribute is `maxRetries`, and total attempts are one
 plus its value; and the enabling annotation is `@EnableResilientMethods`,
 not the older `@EnableRetry`. The Resilience4j artifact targets Boot 3.x, and
 Spring Retry is superseded by the core support. A circuit breaker is
 omitted separately and for its own reason: the only synchronous hops
 between services stay inside the private network behind an internal load
 balancer with bounded connect and read timeouts, so a breaker would add a
 failure mode without removing one. The durable retry tier is queue
 redelivery into a dead letter queue plus per state orchestration retry,
 neither of which is a library concern at all.

 NO AWS SDK, no JPA, no JDBC driver, no Flyway, no actuator, and no cache or
 streaming client.
 Assumptions: this module owns no schema, no entity and no transport; the
 eight services own those, one schema each. Keeping the AWS SDK off the
 shared kernel's classpath is what makes the layering rule structural rather
 than aspirational, because `LayeringRulesTest` forbids AWS SDK and web
 types inside any `..domain..` package and a rule is far easier to hold when
 the forbidden types are not sitting on every consumer's classpath to begin
 with. Redis, Kafka and Kinesis are absent because the baseline has no cache
 tier and its messaging requirement is request and reply, which a queue
 satisfies. Actuator is absent for a simpler reason: a library exposes no
 health endpoint.

 NO `repositories` and no `pluginRepositories` element.
 Assumptions: every artifact declared above resolves from Maven Central.
 This project has no private or internal registry and introduces none, so no
 build here depends on a credentialed settings file. The one registry the
 project writes to is its own container registry, reached by short lived
 federated role assumption from the deployment pipeline and never from a
 build descriptor.

 NO `properties` element and no `build` element.
 Assumptions: the Java release, the compiler settings and every plugin
 binding come from the aggregator, and this module overrides none of them.
 Both elements are omitted entirely rather than left empty, because an empty
 element carries no information and invites the next reader to fill it in.
=============================================================================
  -->

</project>
